{"reference":"Galatians 1","book":"Galatians","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The opening moves unusually quickly from grace, peace, and praise to Paul’s astonishment that the assemblies are turning toward a different message. The missing opening thanksgiving heightens the urgency of the conflict, while the continued family language shows that rebuke does not erase Paul’s concern for his readers.","verse_refs":["Galatians 1:1-10","Galatians 1:3-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_galatians_001_ff5645fe12815cd83ca5e7d4"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"A forceful warning","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Paul repeats his curse on anyone who proclaims a message contrary to the gospel he received and preached. The repetition marks a boundary around that proclamation; it is polemical rhetoric, not authorization for violence against people who dissent.","verse_refs":["Galatians 1:8-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_galatians_001_8cc0cd5414957d661f822383"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Who were the Galatians?","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"“The churches of Galatia” refers to multiple assemblies, but the letter gives no city names. “Galatia” could be understood as an ethnic region or as the larger Roman province, so North- and South-Galatian destination proposals remain disputed. The chapter does not permit a reconstructed list of recipient cities or a uniform picture of the readers’ ethnicity, social position, or way of life.","verse_refs":["Galatians 1:2"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_galatians_001_6d232ac6eebf0f5904bf333a"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"chronology","title":"Paul’s selective account","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Paul presents his call and movements as an account of independence from immediate human consultation: Arabia, Damascus, then—after three years—a fifteen-day visit with Cephas in Jerusalem, where he also saw James. He then names Syria and Cilicia, while noting that the Judean assemblies knew him only through reports. This supplies relative sequence and limited contact, not a complete travel diary.","verse_refs":["Galatians 1:11-17","Galatians 1:18-20","Galatians 1:21-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_galatians_001_6532ee717e6ef44c63812215"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Autobiography under dispute","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Paul’s story includes both his former persecution of God’s assembly and his present preaching. His oath that he is not lying signals that this autobiography is contested. It strengthens the point that these are his own assertions, while not independently verifying every detail of his itinerary or allowing later status to erase the harm he describes.","verse_refs":["Galatians 1:13-24","Galatians 1:20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_galatians_001_5a19e3bb810ec9e0c759fbf7","reader_path_galatians_001_70b0d390339f0d64e80fdb55"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading alongside Acts","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"When this account is compared with Acts, the two narratives differ in selection, emphasis, and sequence around Paul’s call and Jerusalem contacts. They should be compared passage by passage: neither automatic harmonization nor automatic contradiction settles their relationship.","verse_refs":["Galatians 2:1-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_galatians_001_5992a5a992bd65423af188b4","reader_path_galatians_001_9550f9e28c9c0d45d3b2e285"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}